.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-CHECK-PERMISSION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-check-permission \- ask whether a user may do something, and why
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam check-permission
.BR \-\-user | \-u
.I USERID
.BR \-\-target | \-t
.I MODULE
.BR \-\-action | \-a
.I ACTION
[\fB\-\-namespace\fR|\fB\-e\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-\-resource\fR|\fB\-r\fR \fIERN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Answers whether a user would be allowed to do something, and says what
decided it. Nothing is done and nothing is changed.
.PP
It evaluates what a real request would: every grant held by the user and
by every group they belong to, filtered to the account, the namespace and
the resource, then matched against what each grant's role holds. This is
the command that turns "they say they cannot publish" into an answer.
.SH WHY IT IS NOT JUST LIST-GRANTS
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1)
with
.B \-\-principal
shows a user's
.I own
grants. Most rights in a working installation arrive through group
membership, and those do not appear there. This command counts them.
.PP
It also applies the parts of the decision that a list cannot show: whether
the grant's namespaces cover the one asked about, whether its resource
patterns cover the resource, and whether the role actually holds the
permission \- including through a
.I <module>:*
or
.I *:*
wildcard.
.SH THE ANSWER
.TP
.B allowed
The verdict.
.TP
.B reason
One line saying what decided it. On an allow, which role granted it and
to which principal \- so a right that arrived through a group names the
group. On a refusal, one of three things:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.I "no grant applies to this caller in account ..., namespace ..."
\- nothing was in scope at all. Usually a missing grant, or the right one
scoped to a different namespace.
.IP \(bu 3
.I "no role granted here holds '<permission>'"
\- something applies and does not cover this action. Usually the wrong
role.
.IP \(bu 3
.I "'<permission>' is not a permission any role can hold"
\- the module or action does not exist, or belongs to
.I emm
or
.IR emd ,
which nothing can grant. A misspelling reads like this rather than like a
missing grant. See
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-permissions (1).
.RE
.TP
.B role
The role whose grant allowed it. Empty on a refusal.
.PP
The two questions "is it allowed" and "why" are answered together on
purpose: a refusal is the case worth explaining, and an operator reading
one needs to know which of the three it is before they know what to
change.
.SH THE TWO SHORT-CIRCUITS
An installation administrator \- a member of the
.I administrator
user group \- is allowed everything without holding a single grant, and
the answer says so rather than inventing a role. This is why an
administrator's own access cannot be debugged with this command: the
answer is always yes.
.PP
An action outside the permission vocabulary is refused before any grant
is looked at. That is what keeps
.I emm
and
.I emd
unreachable: no permission of theirs exists to be required.
.SH WHICH ACCOUNT IT ASKS ABOUT
The subject's own. The account comes from the user named by
.BR \-\-user ,
not from an option, so this asks what that user may do where they live.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSERID\fR
The user to ask about \- a user id, not an ERN. Required.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target " " \fIMODULE\fR
Module, e.g. \fIens\fR. Required.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-action " " \fIACTION\fR
Action, e.g. \fIpublish\-message\fR. The two together are the permission
\- the same pair the request itself carries as
.I x\-euclid\-target
and
.IR x\-euclid\-action .
Required.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-namespace " " \fINAME\fR
Namespace to ask about. Empty \- the default \- is the account root,
which only a grant scoped to
.I *
or to the empty namespace covers. To ask about a namespace, name it.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-resource " " \fIERN\fR
The resource, for actions that name one. Left out, the grant's resource
patterns do not narrow the answer \- which is right for an action that
names no resource, and optimistic for one that does. Name the resource
when there is one.
.SH WHO MAY RUN IT
Installation administrators \- members of the
.I administrator
user group. See
.B ADMINISTRATOR PRIVILEGES
in
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The question was answered. A refusal is a successful answer \- read
.IR allowed ,
not the exit status.
.TP
.B 1
A required option was missing, no user of that id, or the caller is not
an administrator. If the failure was an expired or invalid session
(\fIHTTP 401\fR), the stored credentials file is removed; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Why can't they publish?
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam check-permission \e
    --user jdoe --target ens --action publish-message \e
    --namespace production \e
    --resource ern:ens:eu-central-1:000000000000:production:topic:orders

# The same question with no resource named - answers what the role and
# namespace allow, before the resource patterns are applied
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam check-permission \e
    --user jdoe --target ens --action publish-message \e
    --namespace production

# Does the group membership actually carry what it was supposed to?
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam check-permission \e
    --user svc-orders --target eqs --action receive-messages \e
    --namespace production
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-permissions (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-user-group-add-user (1)
